Let lookup() release the usrloc slot before freeing the AOR it locked by.

lookup() locks the udomain slot by the AOR it builds in pkg memory, and on
both ways out frees that AOR first and unlocks after:

	pkg_free(aor.s);
	ul.unlock_udomain(_d, &aor);

unlock_udomain() finds the slot by hashing aor (core_hash()), so it hashes
freed memory. Once that memory is reused before the unlock - another
process cannot do it, but a pkg allocator that writes into freed fragments
can - the hash lands on a different slot: the wrong lock is released and the
IMPU's stays held, which wedges every later request for any IMPU in that
slot. memcheck with system malloc (-X sm) reports it on every terminating
request: "Invalid read of size 4 at core_hash, by unlock_udomain, by lookup".

Unlock first, then free.

diff --git a/src/modules/ims_registrar_scscf/lookup.c b/src/modules/ims_registrar_scscf/lookup.c
index 46122054ae..25af84aace 100644
--- a/src/modules/ims_registrar_scscf/lookup.c
+++ b/src/modules/ims_registrar_scscf/lookup.c
@@ -133,8 +133,8 @@ int lookup(struct sip_msg *_m, udomain_t *_d, char *ue_type_c)
 	res = ul.get_impurecord(_d, &aor, &r);
 	if(res != 0) {
 		LM_DBG("'%.*s' Not found in usrloc\n", aor.len, ZSW(aor.s));
-		pkg_free(aor.s);
 		ul.unlock_udomain(_d, &aor);
+		pkg_free(aor.s);
 		return -1;
 	}
 	ret = -1;
@@ -247,8 +247,9 @@ int lookup(struct sip_msg *_m, udomain_t *_d, char *ue_type_c)
 	}
 
 done:
-	pkg_free(aor.s);
+	/* the lock is found by hashing aor: free it only once released */
 	ul.unlock_udomain(_d, &aor);
+	pkg_free(aor.s);
 	return ret;
 }
 
